  "answer_text": "The next NBA game is **Game 3 of the 2026 NBA Finals** between the **San Antonio Spurs and the New York Knicks**. \n\nBecause the series is tied to the current postseason, the game is scheduled for **Monday, June 8, 2026, at 8:30 PM ET** at **Madison Square Garden** in New York City. The Knicks currently lead the series 2-0.\n\nDue to the historic nature of this matchup\u2014marking the Knicks\u2019 first home Finals game in 27 years\u2014demand is incredibly high, and resale prices have reached record-breaking levels. \n\nIf you are looking to purchase tickets, you can check the following platforms:\n\n### 1. Official & Primary Marketplace\n* **Ticketmaster:** As the official ticket marketplace of the NBA, this is the first place to look for verified resale tickets directly integrated with the venue's ticketing system.\n\n### 2. Major Secondary Resale Sites\nBecause the game is sold out from the box office, secondary brokers are your primary option:\n* **TickPick:** Highly recommended for comparing true costs because they do not charge buyers any hidden service fees. *(Note: As of this weekend, the \"get-in\" price for the cheapest ticket on TickPick is hovering just under $10,000.)*\n* **StubHub:** One of the largest resale platforms. Tickets are 100% guaranteed under their FanProtect program.\n* **SeatGeek:** Another highly reliable major ticket search engine and secondary marketplace.\n* **Vivid Seats:** Widely used and often features competitive pricing compared to other major marketplaces.\n* **Gametime:** Excellent for last-minute buyers, though given the extreme demand for the Finals, prices are unlikely to drop significantly right before tip-off.\n\n### Tips Before You Buy:\n* **Compare the Final Screen Price:** Many sites (except TickPick) hide substantial service and delivery fees until the very final payment screen. Always toggle \"Show prices with fees\" when browsing to see the true cost.\n* **Beware of Scams:** Avoid buying tickets from individuals on social media or unregulated classified sites (like Craigslist). Because of the extreme ticket values for these Finals games, scammers are highly active. Stick strictly to verified platforms that offer buyer guarantees.",
